<article class="env-lemma" id="05GI">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05GI">Lemma <span data-tag="05GI">10.96.6</span>.</a> Let $R$ be a ring, let $I \subset R$ be an ideal, and let $R^\wedge = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits R/I^ n$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
any element of $R^\wedge $ which maps to a unit of $R/I$ is a unit, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
any element of $1 + I$ maps to an invertible element of $R^\wedge $, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
any element of $1 + IR^\wedge $ is invertible in $R^\wedge $, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
the ideals $IR^\wedge $ and $\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(R^\wedge \to R/I)$ are contained in the Jacobson radical of $R^\wedge $. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $x \in R^\wedge $ map to a unit $x_1$ in $R/I$. Then $x$ maps to a unit $x_ n$ in $R/I^ n$ for every $n$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0AMG" data-tag="0AMG">10.32.4</a>. Hence $y = (x_ n^{-1}) \in \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits R/I^ n = R^\wedge $ is an inverse to $x$. Parts (2) and (3) follow immediately from (1). Part (4) follows from (1) and Lemma <a href="/tag/0AME" data-tag="0AME">10.19.1</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>